1
00:01:12,839 --> 00:01:14,637
Still messing with my uke?

2
00:01:14,808 --> 00:01:15,832
I'm not messing with it.

3
00:01:16,009 --> 00:01:18,979
I'm just, you know, trying to
teach myself how to play it.

4
00:01:19,146 --> 00:01:21,240
- Well, you have to make a chord.
- Chord?

5
00:01:21,415 --> 00:01:23,455
Take this finger right
there, put it on that string.

6
00:01:23,584 --> 00:01:24,711
- Like that? Okay.
- Yeah.

7
00:01:24,885 --> 00:01:26,805
- Take that finger and put it there.
- Like that?

8
00:01:26,954 --> 00:01:28,479
All right, now, play the four.

9
00:01:28,655 --> 00:01:30,248
- The four of them?
- Mm-hm.

10
00:01:30,891 --> 00:01:32,257
See? That's the C chord.

11
00:01:32,426 --> 00:01:33,485
- Hey!
- Ha-ha.

12
00:01:33,660 --> 00:01:35,356
- How about that?
- Heh-heh.

13
00:01:35,529 --> 00:01:38,863
- Did Will call?
- No, he didn't.

14
00:01:40,734 --> 00:01:42,032
He's late.

15
00:01:42,202 --> 00:01:45,070
Well... Well, he's
only four minutes late.

16
00:01:45,238 --> 00:01:47,571
I can't wait to get
out to that lake.

17
00:01:47,741 --> 00:01:49,039
We've already picked out

18
00:01:49,209 --> 00:01:51,337
- our favorite spot and everything.
- Oh...

19
00:01:52,879 --> 00:01:54,404
Will you get that?

20
00:01:54,581 --> 00:01:55,742
- Yeah. Oh, yeah.
- Careful.

21
00:01:55,916 --> 00:01:57,316
I'll treat it as if
it were my own.

22
00:01:57,451 --> 00:01:58,680
- Yeah.
- There you go.

23
00:02:00,487 --> 00:02:01,955
Coming.

24
00:02:08,595 --> 00:02:11,588
- Oh, Ben.
- Hey, Ben.

25
00:02:11,765 --> 00:02:14,394
- What happened?
- Well, I got mugged last night.

26
00:02:14,801 --> 00:02:16,770
- What?
- Well, I don't know who it was.

27
00:02:16,937 --> 00:02:19,257
Came up behind me and whacked
me on the arm with a crowbar,

28
00:02:19,406 --> 00:02:20,806
took my wallet and ran off.

29
00:02:21,775 --> 00:02:23,266
Well, that's awful.

30
00:02:23,443 --> 00:02:26,436
Ben, I know how much you
really wanted to go on this trip.

31
00:02:26,613 --> 00:02:29,674
If I thought I could
fish left-handed, heh...

32
00:02:29,850 --> 00:02:31,876
Well, there's always next year.

33
00:02:32,052 --> 00:02:33,486
I'm sorry, Ben.

34
00:02:33,654 --> 00:02:35,122
Take care of your arm.

35
00:02:35,288 --> 00:02:37,985
- Thank you.
- Yeah, I'll see you, Will.

36
00:02:38,158 --> 00:02:40,684
- See you.
- See you, Will.

37
00:02:41,128 --> 00:02:42,687
Oh...

38
00:02:43,730 --> 00:02:45,858
That's gotta hurt.

39
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:48,964
What do you look
so dejected for?

40
00:02:49,136 --> 00:02:51,264
You know, you
can still go fishing.

41
00:02:51,672 --> 00:02:53,766
I don't wanna fish by myself.

42
00:02:53,940 --> 00:02:55,806
Oh, don't you remember?
I told you yesterday.

43
00:02:55,976 --> 00:02:57,569
One of Judge Stern's
law clerks called.

44
00:02:57,744 --> 00:03:00,805
He invited you to go
out on his boat tomorrow.

45
00:03:01,982 --> 00:03:04,577
You know, Judge Stern's
invited me a hundred times

46
00:03:04,751 --> 00:03:06,413
and I've never been able to go.

47
00:03:06,586 --> 00:03:08,578
Well, give him a call.

48
00:03:08,755 --> 00:03:10,417
- Hi, guys.
- Oh, hey, Jer.

49
00:03:10,590 --> 00:03:13,560
Well, uh, I thought you'd
be long gone by now.

50
00:03:13,727 --> 00:03:14,751
Tomorrow.

51
00:03:15,395 --> 00:03:17,830
I'm going fishing on
Judge Stern's boat.

52
00:03:19,132 --> 00:03:21,033
I thought you got
seasick on boats.

53
00:03:21,835 --> 00:03:24,964
Oh, no, that was
a long time ago.

54
00:03:53,734 --> 00:03:54,758
- Ben.
- Huh?

55
00:03:54,935 --> 00:03:57,962
Here's some soda water. Drink
some of that and you'll be all right.

56
00:03:58,138 --> 00:04:00,403
- Get your sea legs back in no time.
- Okay.

57
00:04:00,574 --> 00:04:03,066
Now, Ben, would you
mind if I went back out there

58
00:04:03,243 --> 00:04:04,677
for about 30 minutes?

59
00:04:04,845 --> 00:04:07,007
My boat's been in dry dock
for a week getting painted,

60
00:04:07,180 --> 00:04:09,376
and I sure would like
to do a little more fishing.

61
00:04:09,549 --> 00:04:11,347
- Go ahead.
- You'll be all right?

62
00:04:11,518 --> 00:04:13,043
I'll be all right.

63
00:05:15,215 --> 00:05:16,649
- Glen Silver?
- Yeah.

64
00:05:16,817 --> 00:05:18,843
I'm Detective Marks.
This is Officer Brown.

65
00:05:19,019 --> 00:05:21,921
I'd like to ask you
a few questions.

66
00:05:22,088 --> 00:05:23,716
What's this about, detective?

67
00:05:23,890 --> 00:05:25,756
Were you at the
marina yesterday?

68
00:05:25,926 --> 00:05:27,554
- Yeah. Why?
- Did you happen to see

69
00:05:27,727 --> 00:05:29,491
Judge Michael Stern
while you were there?

70
00:05:29,663 --> 00:05:31,564
Judge Stern?

71
00:05:31,731 --> 00:05:33,971
- What are you talking about?
- Straightforward question.

72
00:05:34,134 --> 00:05:36,574
Did you see Judge Stern yesterday
when you were at the marina?

73
00:05:36,736 --> 00:05:39,103
I'm not answering till you
tell me what this is about.

74
00:05:39,272 --> 00:05:42,333
Judge Stern was killed in an
explosion on his boat yesterday.

75
00:05:45,846 --> 00:05:47,644
I haven't seen the
judge in over four years.

76
00:05:49,282 --> 00:05:50,443
Why are you asking me this?

77
00:05:50,617 --> 00:05:51,915
We found your fingerprints

78
00:05:52,085 --> 00:05:54,145
near the slip where
the judge kept his boat.

79
00:05:54,521 --> 00:05:55,648
- What?
- What do you say

80
00:05:55,822 --> 00:05:57,916
we talk a little more
about this downtown?

81
00:06:05,832 --> 00:06:07,494
Hi, Mr. Matlock.

82
00:06:07,667 --> 00:06:09,033
Oh, hi, Rick.

83
00:06:09,202 --> 00:06:10,761
I'm still in a state of shock.

84
00:06:10,937 --> 00:06:13,031
Yeah, we all are.

85
00:06:13,273 --> 00:06:15,003
- This is, uh...
- Oh, I'm Cliff Lewis.

86
00:06:15,175 --> 00:06:17,872
Hi. Rick Petkin.
Judge Stern's law clerk.

87
00:06:18,044 --> 00:06:20,964
You know, I didn't even think the
judge was taking his boat out this week.

88
00:06:21,114 --> 00:06:23,447
He said he was
getting it painted.

89
00:06:24,251 --> 00:06:26,311
Well, if he wasn't
gonna take the boat out,

90
00:06:26,486 --> 00:06:31,390
why did you call Cliff and tell
me he wanted me to go fishing?

91
00:06:31,558 --> 00:06:33,823
You know, you don't
sound like the guy I talked to.

92
00:06:33,994 --> 00:06:35,656
I wasn't. I didn't call you.

93
00:06:36,463 --> 00:06:38,762
Well, maybe it was one of
Judge Stern's other law clerks.

94
00:06:38,932 --> 00:06:41,060
No, I'm it.

95
00:06:41,968 --> 00:06:43,128
Now, Ben, I'm not delusional.

96
00:06:43,270 --> 00:06:46,035
I know someone from Judge
Stern's law office called me up,

97
00:06:46,206 --> 00:06:49,540
said they were his clerk, and
invited you out on that boat.

98
00:06:51,244 --> 00:06:55,011
Well, somebody
wanted me on that boat.

99
00:06:56,516 --> 00:06:59,918
Why are you so interested
in the guy who mugged me?

100
00:07:00,086 --> 00:07:04,854
Well, Will, I got a kind of a sick
feeling that the explosion on that boat

101
00:07:05,025 --> 00:07:06,857
was meant for me
and not the judge.

102
00:07:07,027 --> 00:07:08,461
Get out of here.

103
00:07:08,628 --> 00:07:10,290
And if it was meant for me,

104
00:07:10,463 --> 00:07:14,628
then somebody had to know
that you and I were going fishing.

105
00:07:14,801 --> 00:07:16,963
In order to get me on that boat,

106
00:07:17,137 --> 00:07:19,697
they had to make sure
we did not go to the lake.

107
00:07:19,873 --> 00:07:21,273
What are you saying, Ben?

108
00:07:21,441 --> 00:07:23,740
I mean, the guy who
broke my arm did it

109
00:07:23,910 --> 00:07:26,004
just so that you'd go
fishing with somebody else?

110
00:07:26,680 --> 00:07:29,206
You didn't even get a glimpse
of the guy that whacked you?

111
00:07:29,382 --> 00:07:31,578
He told me if I
turned around, I'd die.

112
00:07:31,751 --> 00:07:33,049
And I didn't wanna die.

113
00:07:33,219 --> 00:07:35,415
Hm. Yeah.

114
00:07:35,722 --> 00:07:37,088
What did his voice sound like?

115
00:07:37,257 --> 00:07:40,853
Well, it was kind of raspy, I
remember, and a little high.

116
00:07:45,999 --> 00:07:48,161
I'd gotten a call from the
head of Human Resources

117
00:07:48,335 --> 00:07:50,531
- at D.C. Drake Corp.
- Hm.

118
00:07:50,704 --> 00:07:53,230
He said his name
was, uh, Don Arnold.

119
00:07:53,406 --> 00:07:56,240
The company sells
boating equipment.

120
00:07:56,543 --> 00:07:58,774
Well, he said a friend of
mine recommended me

121
00:07:58,945 --> 00:08:00,277
for a sales job in the company

122
00:08:00,447 --> 00:08:03,884
and he wanted to discuss it
over lunch down at the marina.

123
00:08:04,284 --> 00:08:06,412
Did you call your friend
to verify if he did it?

124
00:08:08,521 --> 00:08:10,961
I wasn't about to look a gift
horse in the mouth, Mr. Matlock.

125
00:08:11,124 --> 00:08:13,423
I haven't worked in a long time.

126
00:08:13,593 --> 00:08:14,891
So you met him for lunch?

127
00:08:15,695 --> 00:08:18,597
Yeah. He seemed like a nice guy.

128
00:08:18,765 --> 00:08:21,166
- So much for my judge of character.
- Hm.

129
00:08:21,334 --> 00:08:24,998
So we had lunch and we
took a walk down by the dock.

130
00:08:25,538 --> 00:08:27,370
What did he look like?

131
00:08:27,540 --> 00:08:30,669
Nice-looking guy,
very muscular, um,

132
00:08:30,844 --> 00:08:32,312
blond hair.

133
00:08:32,479 --> 00:08:35,347
- Looked like he'd just gotten it cut.
- Hm.

134
00:08:35,515 --> 00:08:37,143
What about his voice?

135
00:08:38,585 --> 00:08:41,054
It's funny you
should mention that.

136
00:08:41,988 --> 00:08:45,925
I remember thinking that
he had a very high voice.

137
00:08:49,996 --> 00:08:53,433
Mr. Matlock, five years ago,
when Judge Stern had me disbarred

138
00:08:53,600 --> 00:08:56,959
for jury tampering, I hated him.

139
00:08:57,504 --> 00:08:59,405
I was very vocal about it.

140
00:08:59,572 --> 00:09:01,734
I was just angry
that I got caught.

141
00:09:01,908 --> 00:09:05,675
The years of law school,
the indignity of it all and...

142
00:09:06,379 --> 00:09:10,510
I know you didn't
kill the judge, Glen.

143
00:09:10,683 --> 00:09:14,017
But whoever framed you
really did his homework,

144
00:09:14,454 --> 00:09:18,824
killing the judge on the
anniversary of your disbarment.

145
00:09:18,992 --> 00:09:21,359
Somebody must have really
wanted to kill Judge Stern.

146
00:09:23,029 --> 00:09:24,520
Unless I miss my guess,

147
00:09:24,697 --> 00:09:26,962
this had nothing to
do with Judge Stern.

148
00:09:27,767 --> 00:09:30,396
- What?
- I think whoever blew up that boat

149
00:09:30,570 --> 00:09:33,734
had something else
in mind altogether.

150
00:09:34,407 --> 00:09:35,966
Killing me.

151
00:09:45,585 --> 00:09:48,077
If somebody wanted you
dead, Ben, wouldn't it be easier

152
00:09:48,254 --> 00:09:50,951
just to shoot you down or
poison you or something?

153
00:09:51,124 --> 00:09:52,353
What a way to talk.

154
00:09:52,525 --> 00:09:54,426
Oh, you know what I'm saying.

155
00:09:54,594 --> 00:09:56,790
He would've had to
make all those phone calls,

156
00:09:56,963 --> 00:10:00,365
pretend to be a law clerk,
get you on the judge's boat

157
00:10:00,533 --> 00:10:02,729
and then frame Glen Silver.

158
00:10:02,902 --> 00:10:04,632
Mm, mm, mm. Uh-uh.

159
00:10:04,804 --> 00:10:06,500
I think it had to be
more than one person.

160
00:10:06,673 --> 00:10:08,073
You know, think about it.

161
00:10:08,241 --> 00:10:10,710
Because this muscular
guy with this high voice,

162
00:10:10,877 --> 00:10:14,006
he would've had to have
attacked Will, set up Glen Silver

163
00:10:14,180 --> 00:10:17,150
and kill Judge Stern.
It doesn't make sense.

164
00:10:17,317 --> 00:10:18,797
You tell me how
many killers out there

165
00:10:18,952 --> 00:10:21,148
would take a guy to lunch
they're gonna be framing.

166
00:10:22,555 --> 00:10:24,023
I think the guy
Glen had lunch with

167
00:10:24,624 --> 00:10:27,219
works for whoever
blew up the judge's boat.

168
00:10:27,393 --> 00:10:29,692
- Mm, yeah. Yeah.
- A long calculated plan

169
00:10:29,863 --> 00:10:32,162
- that took months to get together.
- Mm.

170
00:10:32,332 --> 00:10:34,062
I've got a lot of enemies.

171
00:10:34,234 --> 00:10:37,204
Fortunately, most of
them are in jail. Ha-ha-ha.

172
00:10:37,370 --> 00:10:39,498
What if one of them
isn't, you know?

173
00:10:39,672 --> 00:10:41,300
What if someone's
on parole or escaped?

174
00:10:41,474 --> 00:10:42,840
Let me look into
that, all right?

175
00:10:43,243 --> 00:10:45,974
Well, yeah, thanks. Yeah.

176
00:10:48,047 --> 00:10:50,448
Yeah, I've been checking out
all the barbershops in the area.

177
00:10:50,617 --> 00:10:53,348
Muscular guy with blond
hair and a high voice?

178
00:10:53,520 --> 00:10:55,320
Mm-hm. He might have
gotten his hair cut here,

179
00:10:55,455 --> 00:10:57,788
like, last Tuesday or Wednesday?

180
00:10:59,459 --> 00:11:03,191
- Is something wrong?
- No. Your hair is just a little stringy.

181
00:11:05,498 --> 00:11:07,091
My hair is not stringy.

182
00:11:07,500 --> 00:11:10,020
Do you remember cutting a
guy's hair that fits that description?

183
00:11:10,170 --> 00:11:12,935
No, but one of my guys is
out sick with the flu today.

184
00:11:13,106 --> 00:11:16,372
- He might have cut it. I could call him.
- Well, here's my business card.

185
00:11:16,543 --> 00:11:18,205
You can call me at that number.

186
00:11:20,680 --> 00:11:22,012
- What?
- Nothing.

187
00:11:22,182 --> 00:11:25,516
Uh, you really like bangs, huh?

188
00:11:25,685 --> 00:11:27,244
They'll probably
make a comeback.

189
00:11:34,661 --> 00:11:37,995
Ben, what do you think
of the way I wear my hair?

190
00:11:38,164 --> 00:11:39,496
It's okay.

191
00:11:39,666 --> 00:11:40,690
Okay?

192
00:11:40,867 --> 00:11:42,893
Yeah, I like
old-fashioned hairdos.

193
00:11:43,636 --> 00:11:47,437
Ben, Ben, Wayne Drummond was
released from prison four months ago.

194
00:11:47,607 --> 00:11:49,473
- Who's Wayne Drummond?
- Oh, he got out, huh?

195
00:11:49,642 --> 00:11:51,682
Yeah. He must have had a
friend on the parole board.

196
00:11:51,811 --> 00:11:53,611
- Who's Wayne Drummond?
- He really hates him.

197
00:11:53,746 --> 00:11:55,214
Who's Wayne Drummond?

198
00:11:56,049 --> 00:12:01,886
Uh, he's a bad actor
who turned killer.

199
00:12:02,055 --> 00:12:03,648
Oh, that Wayne Drummond.

200
00:12:03,823 --> 00:12:09,126
A while back, Leanne and I
stayed at the Wilmont Hotel

201
00:12:09,295 --> 00:12:11,730
for a cousin's wedding.

202
00:12:12,332 --> 00:12:15,131
<i>It turns out I got sick
as a dog that night</i>

203
00:12:15,301 --> 00:12:18,396
<i>and spent the whole
weekend in my hotel room.</i>

204
00:12:18,571 --> 00:12:21,302
<i>I was having trouble
sleeping that first night</i>

205
00:12:21,474 --> 00:12:25,741
<i>and just by chance took a
peek outside my window.</i>

206
00:12:25,912 --> 00:12:31,818
<i>In a house across from the hotel, I
saw a young guy fighting with a girl.</i>

207
00:12:31,985 --> 00:12:34,477
<i>They were at each
other pretty good.</i>

208
00:12:34,654 --> 00:12:35,781
<i>But it was their business,</i>

209
00:12:35,955 --> 00:12:38,948
<i>and I wasn't about
to stick my nose in it.</i>

210
00:12:58,278 --> 00:13:01,442
<i>A little while later, I
happened by the window again,</i>

211
00:13:01,614 --> 00:13:06,575
<i>and I saw what turned out to be
this girl's husband coming home.</i>

212
00:13:06,753 --> 00:13:08,483
<i>The moment he drove up,</i>

213
00:13:08,655 --> 00:13:11,022
<i>the boy inside
hightailed it out of there.</i>

214
00:13:31,644 --> 00:13:36,810
<i>The next morning, I found
out the girl had been murdered.</i>

215
00:13:36,983 --> 00:13:40,476
What in the world is
going on down there?

216
00:13:40,653 --> 00:13:42,884
I don't know.

217
00:13:43,056 --> 00:13:46,424
But that's the house I was
telling you about last night

218
00:13:46,592 --> 00:13:48,185
where I saw that fight.

219
00:13:49,762 --> 00:13:52,755
Looks like the
medical examiner's car.

220
00:13:58,938 --> 00:14:01,100
And so you were
acquainted with the deceased,

221
00:14:01,274 --> 00:14:04,108
- were you?
- Yeah, but...

222
00:14:05,745 --> 00:14:09,045
- Who died?
- Helene Cameron.

223
00:14:09,215 --> 00:14:12,242
Uh, dark hair, mid-30s?

224
00:14:13,319 --> 00:14:14,753
Yeah.

225
00:14:14,921 --> 00:14:18,119
The police say she
was stabbed in the back.

226
00:14:18,291 --> 00:14:19,589
Dad.

227
00:14:21,661 --> 00:14:23,095
I think that's her husband.

228
00:14:23,262 --> 00:14:25,254
He's being arrested.

229
00:14:26,032 --> 00:14:29,025
That's not the man I saw
fighting with her last night.

230
00:14:29,202 --> 00:14:31,762
- It's not?
- No.

231
00:14:32,372 --> 00:14:34,364
I think they got the wrong guy.

232
00:14:35,274 --> 00:14:36,434
You're looking mighty pretty.

233
00:14:36,576 --> 00:14:39,444
<i>The kid I'd seen fighting
with Helene Cameron</i>

234
00:14:39,612 --> 00:14:42,377
<i>was Wayne Drummond,
a wannabe actor.</i>

235
00:14:42,548 --> 00:14:44,710
<i>When I saw him rehearsing
the play he was in,</i>

236
00:14:44,884 --> 00:14:48,116
<i>well, I got a sinking
feeling in my stomach.</i>

237
00:14:48,287 --> 00:14:49,812
Oh!

238
00:14:51,290 --> 00:14:52,553
Dad.

239
00:14:54,026 --> 00:14:56,757
- They were rehearsing that scene.
- Yeah.

240
00:14:57,764 --> 00:15:00,393
Mr. Drummond, I'd like you to
meet my father, Ben Matlock.

241
00:15:00,566 --> 00:15:01,898
- Hi.
- I saw you

242
00:15:02,068 --> 00:15:05,561
at, uh, Helene Cameron's
house the night she died.

243
00:15:05,738 --> 00:15:08,298
Were you working on this scene?

244
00:15:08,474 --> 00:15:11,638
Yeah. That's her understudy.

245
00:15:11,811 --> 00:15:17,944
I guess that's what made it,
uh, look like you were fighting.

246
00:15:18,117 --> 00:15:19,415
The scene.

247
00:15:19,585 --> 00:15:20,985
How much did you see?

248
00:15:21,154 --> 00:15:23,919
Did Helene break
up with you that night?

249
00:15:29,128 --> 00:15:30,323
- No.
- Did she tell you

250
00:15:30,496 --> 00:15:32,658
she was never
gonna see you again?

251
00:15:32,832 --> 00:15:34,824
We were making plans to
spend the weekend together

252
00:15:35,001 --> 00:15:37,402
when her husband
went out of town.

253
00:15:37,570 --> 00:15:39,937
Who told you she
was going to end it?

254
00:15:40,106 --> 00:15:42,575
Her husband? Is
that what he said?

255
00:15:42,742 --> 00:15:44,335
He said that he and Helene

256
00:15:44,510 --> 00:15:47,241
were gonna put their
marriage back together.

257
00:15:47,413 --> 00:15:48,745
That's a lie.

258
00:15:48,915 --> 00:15:51,612
I loved Helene and she loved me.

259
00:15:51,784 --> 00:15:53,878
She was alive when I left.

260
00:15:54,053 --> 00:15:55,715
<i>Leanne ended up defending</i>

261
00:15:55,888 --> 00:15:58,119
<i>Helene Cameron's
husband, Phillip.</i>

262
00:15:58,291 --> 00:16:01,557
<i>Turned out Wayne Drummond
hated Phillip Cameron</i>

263
00:16:01,727 --> 00:16:05,164
<i>from when he was doing
another play several years ago.</i>

264
00:16:05,731 --> 00:16:08,565
- You killed her, didn't you?
- No, I did not.

265
00:16:08,734 --> 00:16:10,669
Yes, you did.

266
00:16:10,837 --> 00:16:13,898
And for the life of me, I
could not figure out why.

267
00:16:14,073 --> 00:16:16,702
I mean, why would
you do such a thing?

268
00:16:16,876 --> 00:16:20,142
Why would you date a married
woman, over two months ago

269
00:16:20,313 --> 00:16:22,873
take out a life insurance
policy in her husband's name

270
00:16:23,049 --> 00:16:24,210
in order to frame him?

271
00:16:24,383 --> 00:16:26,909
Do I have to listen to these
wild accusations, Your Honor?

272
00:16:27,086 --> 00:16:29,248
Not if she doesn't have proof.

273
00:16:29,422 --> 00:16:30,515
Miss Mclntyre?

274
00:16:30,690 --> 00:16:33,057
- I have proof, Your Honor.
- Proceed.

275
00:16:33,226 --> 00:16:34,986
This play that you
were doing in Porterville,

276
00:16:35,127 --> 00:16:39,292
<i>Cat on a Hot Tin Roof,</i>
it had to close, didn't it?

277
00:16:39,465 --> 00:16:40,626
Yes.

278
00:16:40,800 --> 00:16:43,167
Would you tell the court why?

279
00:16:43,736 --> 00:16:47,036
A few days into the play,
there was a car accident,

280
00:16:47,206 --> 00:16:50,370
and one of the
cast members died.

281
00:16:50,543 --> 00:16:52,637
Her name was Carla Waters.

282
00:16:54,480 --> 00:16:55,914
Yes.

283
00:16:56,082 --> 00:16:57,812
You were in love
with her, weren't you?

284
00:17:00,286 --> 00:17:02,687
And Phillip Cameron was
driving the car that hit her.

285
00:17:02,855 --> 00:17:05,882
Okay, it's true. I was in
love with Carla Waters.

286
00:17:06,058 --> 00:17:08,755
This is a copy of the play

287
00:17:08,928 --> 00:17:12,057
taken this morning by
warrant from your house.

288
00:17:12,965 --> 00:17:15,560
You'll notice that all
of the dialogue in here

289
00:17:15,735 --> 00:17:18,295
that is highlighted
is for Helene's role.

290
00:17:18,938 --> 00:17:21,601
Well, I guess I
picked up her copy.

291
00:17:21,774 --> 00:17:24,903
You'll also notice that on
page 14 there are blood stains,

292
00:17:25,077 --> 00:17:27,546
which the medical
examiner has identified

293
00:17:27,713 --> 00:17:29,045
as Helene Cameron's blood.

294
00:17:29,215 --> 00:17:32,379
Oh, come on, you saw the
scene we had to do in that play,

295
00:17:32,552 --> 00:17:35,112
and so did your father. And
he thought I was killing her.

296
00:17:35,288 --> 00:17:40,727
I remember. I had accidentally hit her
in the nose and she got a nosebleed.

297
00:17:40,893 --> 00:17:45,490
Not according to the medical
examiner who did the autopsy.

298
00:17:45,665 --> 00:17:48,430
There was no blood
anywhere near Helene's face,

299
00:17:48,601 --> 00:17:51,799
no cuts on her body at all,
except for the wound that killed her.

300
00:17:51,971 --> 00:17:54,304
So maybe it happened
the night before.

301
00:17:54,473 --> 00:17:55,998
We rehearsed the
scene for a week.

302
00:17:56,175 --> 00:17:58,337
I... I don't remember.

303
00:17:58,511 --> 00:17:59,877
That's not possible either.

304
00:18:00,046 --> 00:18:01,446
You see, as it turns out,

305
00:18:01,614 --> 00:18:04,209
Helene bought a new script
the morning that she died.

306
00:18:04,383 --> 00:18:07,581
So the only way those blood
stains could've gotten on that script

307
00:18:07,753 --> 00:18:08,812
was when you killed her.

308
00:18:14,493 --> 00:18:18,794
Mr. Drummond, this
was a cold murderous act.

309
00:18:19,532 --> 00:18:22,468
You didn't kill out
of passion or hate.

310
00:18:22,635 --> 00:18:24,934
In order to get back
at Phillip Cameron,

311
00:18:25,104 --> 00:18:28,871
you killed somebody
you didn't even dislike.

312
00:18:32,345 --> 00:18:35,110
Well, he certainly sounds
like the type to hold a grudge.

313
00:18:37,249 --> 00:18:38,581
Hello?

314
00:18:38,751 --> 00:18:40,219
Yeah.

315
00:18:40,953 --> 00:18:42,683
He did.

316
00:18:42,855 --> 00:18:44,790
What was the name?

317
00:18:45,591 --> 00:18:48,117
Great. Thank you. Bye.

318
00:18:48,294 --> 00:18:50,593
- That was my barber friend.
- Hm?

319
00:18:50,763 --> 00:18:53,062
Our soprano muscle
man now has a name.

320
00:18:53,232 --> 00:18:54,723
Ty Foster.

321
00:18:54,900 --> 00:18:58,234
Ty... Ty Foster, huh?

322
00:19:08,614 --> 00:19:11,140
Come on, folks, just stay back.

323
00:19:14,787 --> 00:19:18,246
Mr. Foster! This is the
police! Open the door!

324
00:19:38,978 --> 00:19:41,209
If this is your guy with
the high-pitched voice,

325
00:19:41,380 --> 00:19:42,609
you're not gonna hear it.

326
00:19:53,392 --> 00:19:55,054
Nothing. Not one single lead.

327
00:19:55,227 --> 00:19:56,957
Well, that's the way
it goes sometimes.

328
00:19:57,129 --> 00:19:59,997
Maybe Forensics will come up
with something in the morning.

329
00:20:00,166 --> 00:20:02,965
- See you tomorrow.
- Have a good night.

330
00:21:13,973 --> 00:21:15,305
Did you call the police?

331
00:21:15,474 --> 00:21:17,443
There's no need.

332
00:21:17,610 --> 00:21:19,875
Ben, you gotta call the police.

333
00:21:20,045 --> 00:21:23,072
I know who broke
in here last night.

334
00:21:23,249 --> 00:21:25,445
It was Jeffrey Spidel.

335
00:21:25,618 --> 00:21:29,680
That's why there were no
clues over at Ty Foster's place.

336
00:21:29,855 --> 00:21:31,687
That was him too.

337
00:21:31,857 --> 00:21:35,123
Jeffrey Spidel broke in
this house two years ago

338
00:21:35,294 --> 00:21:37,820
and tried to strangle me.

339
00:21:38,063 --> 00:21:42,433
I just learned this
morning he's out of prison.

340
00:21:48,741 --> 00:21:49,902
Hi.

341
00:21:51,577 --> 00:21:53,512
Jeffrey, what are
you doing here?

342
00:21:54,680 --> 00:21:55,943
You slipped off last night

343
00:21:56,115 --> 00:21:57,795
before I could tell you
to have a good day.

344
00:21:59,819 --> 00:22:01,720
You are something. Heh.

345
00:22:01,887 --> 00:22:03,321
- Yeah.
- Ooh.

346
00:22:03,489 --> 00:22:06,357
- Thank you.
- Hey. Mm...

347
00:22:06,525 --> 00:22:09,984
<i>Jeffrey Spidel was one of
the most charming, likable men</i>

348
00:22:10,162 --> 00:22:12,859
<i>you'd ever wanna meet,
except for one thing.</i>

349
00:22:13,032 --> 00:22:16,002
<i>He was a cold-blooded
serial killer.</i>

350
00:22:22,174 --> 00:22:25,440
- What are you doing?
- I don't wanna leave any fingerprints.

351
00:22:33,185 --> 00:22:34,483
No, Jeffrey!

352
00:22:37,923 --> 00:22:40,950
<i>I met him after he
strangled Patricia Downey,</i>

353
00:22:41,126 --> 00:22:43,357
<i>a senior partner in
my accountant's firm.</i>

354
00:22:43,529 --> 00:22:46,556
<i>I figured Spidel must
have really hated her.</i>

355
00:22:46,732 --> 00:22:47,859
<i>I was wrong.</i>

356
00:22:48,033 --> 00:22:51,367
<i>The person he
really hated was me.</i>

357
00:22:56,342 --> 00:22:59,222
- Yes, we had a wonderful evening.
- Sure you didn't have a big argument?

358
00:22:59,378 --> 00:23:00,676
Of course I'm sure.

359
00:23:00,846 --> 00:23:02,290
She didn't lock herself
in the bathroom?

360
00:23:02,314 --> 00:23:05,751
- Where did you get such an idea?
- I've got to ask you where you were

361
00:23:05,918 --> 00:23:07,853
the morning Patricia
Downey was murdered.

362
00:23:09,655 --> 00:23:12,181
I can't believe you...

363
00:23:12,358 --> 00:23:14,884
I really don't have time for
this. I hate to be blunt with you,

364
00:23:15,060 --> 00:23:17,723
but I'm gonna have to ask
you to leave right now. Please.

365
00:23:18,564 --> 00:23:21,466
This makes you
look awfully guilty.

366
00:23:30,609 --> 00:23:33,101
Yes, Mr. Matlock,

367
00:23:33,879 --> 00:23:35,245
I know.

368
00:23:35,414 --> 00:23:39,943
<i>He knew exactly what I'd do
and used it to humiliate me.</i>

369
00:23:40,986 --> 00:23:44,582
And she's all set to testify
that she saw your car

370
00:23:44,757 --> 00:23:46,589
parked in front
of Patricia's house

371
00:23:46,759 --> 00:23:49,251
at 7:15 the morning
she was murdered.

372
00:23:49,428 --> 00:23:53,889
- It couldn't have been my car.
- A black Jaguar XJS convertible

373
00:23:54,066 --> 00:23:55,466
parked right on the street,

374
00:23:55,634 --> 00:23:59,696
just like always, by one of
Patricia's water sprinklers.

375
00:23:59,872 --> 00:24:01,841
That was your car.
You parked it there.

376
00:24:02,007 --> 00:24:05,000
Then you went inside and
you killed her, didn't you?

377
00:24:05,711 --> 00:24:08,306
My car was in the
shop that morning, sir.

378
00:24:11,650 --> 00:24:12,674
What?

379
00:24:12,851 --> 00:24:15,150
My car was in the shop
that morning getting serviced.

380
00:24:15,321 --> 00:24:17,041
They picked it up the
day before the murder,

381
00:24:17,189 --> 00:24:18,680
they returned it to
me the day after.

382
00:24:18,857 --> 00:24:21,918
Call Doug Harding Motors.
They keep records too, you know.

383
00:24:25,064 --> 00:24:27,465
I have no idea why he
came after me like that.

384
00:24:27,633 --> 00:24:30,626
It was totally uncalled for
and, frankly, a little pathetic.

385
00:24:30,803 --> 00:24:33,238
Mr. Matlock, will you tell
us what just happened?

386
00:24:33,405 --> 00:24:35,397
Where did you get
your information?

387
00:24:35,574 --> 00:24:38,305
- What are you gonna do now?
- Did you check the plate number?

388
00:24:38,477 --> 00:24:40,742
How does it feel to make a
fool of yourself, Mr. Matlock?

389
00:24:40,913 --> 00:24:42,745
Lousy!

390
00:24:42,915 --> 00:24:44,110
Hey.

391
00:24:46,685 --> 00:24:48,950
<i>How does it feel to make a
fool of yourself, Mr. Matlock?</i>

392
00:24:49,121 --> 00:24:51,556
<i>- Lousy!
- Mr. Matlock.</i>

393
00:24:52,858 --> 00:24:55,350
<i>How does it feel to make a
fool of yourself, Mr. Matlock?</i>

394
00:24:55,527 --> 00:24:57,018
<i>- Lousy!
- Lousy.</i>

395
00:25:07,940 --> 00:25:09,966
Saw the news?

396
00:25:10,476 --> 00:25:14,345
The legendary Ben Matlock really
got cut down to size today, didn't he?

397
00:25:14,513 --> 00:25:16,038
Heh-heh.

398
00:25:16,215 --> 00:25:18,810
Being humiliated like
that really hurts, doesn't it?

399
00:25:18,984 --> 00:25:22,318
And you know something? The pain
never goes away. It never goes away.

400
00:25:22,488 --> 00:25:25,720
Every time you look in the mirror,
it comes back like a kick in the gut,

401
00:25:25,891 --> 00:25:27,416
and you're never quite the same.

402
00:25:28,794 --> 00:25:32,629
Councilwoman
Mendez and Bill Davis,

403
00:25:32,798 --> 00:25:36,235
you humiliated them
before you killed them.

404
00:25:36,835 --> 00:25:40,272
Put them out of their misery
is a better way to put it.

405
00:25:42,241 --> 00:25:43,607
They were both lawyers.

406
00:25:43,776 --> 00:25:45,074
Very good.

407
00:25:45,611 --> 00:25:49,241
That mark on their forehead
wasn't the Roman numeral two.

408
00:25:49,415 --> 00:25:51,247
No. No, it's Greek.

409
00:25:51,417 --> 00:25:52,680
Upper case pi.

410
00:25:53,752 --> 00:25:55,516
Plaintiff.

411
00:25:55,888 --> 00:25:58,119
Little touch of irony.

412
00:25:59,058 --> 00:26:00,185
No more talk.

413
00:26:02,161 --> 00:26:03,493
Why lawyers?

414
00:26:04,463 --> 00:26:07,194
Why not? More fun.

415
00:26:14,406 --> 00:26:16,932
- Freeze! Go, go!
- Police officers.

416
00:26:17,476 --> 00:26:19,206
I got him.

417
00:26:22,214 --> 00:26:24,911
Don't move. Just stay
right where you are.

418
00:26:27,786 --> 00:26:31,450
You thought I'd never know
you were coming for me.

419
00:26:34,893 --> 00:26:37,453
But I pretty well had the
whole thing figured out.

420
00:26:43,135 --> 00:26:46,105
- Do you know where he is?
- Yeah.

421
00:26:47,106 --> 00:26:50,167
He's working at The Plant House.

422
00:26:51,043 --> 00:26:53,274
I'm gonna see him today.

423
00:26:54,279 --> 00:26:55,359
Maybe I should go with you.

424
00:26:55,514 --> 00:26:58,746
No. I wanna see him alone.

425
00:27:04,256 --> 00:27:06,851
I heard you were out of jail.

426
00:27:07,025 --> 00:27:08,357
Mr. Matlock.

427
00:27:08,527 --> 00:27:11,156
I didn't know you
were a plant lover.

428
00:27:11,330 --> 00:27:14,027
How'd you get out
on parole so soon?

429
00:27:14,199 --> 00:27:17,931
Well, the appeals court
threw out all the evidence.

430
00:27:18,103 --> 00:27:19,469
I just was reading about you.

431
00:27:19,638 --> 00:27:22,972
You're defending that guy
who blew up that judge's boat

432
00:27:23,142 --> 00:27:24,542
and almost killed you.

433
00:27:24,710 --> 00:27:26,508
He didn't do it.

434
00:27:28,814 --> 00:27:31,750
So you gonna pull one of your
famous rabbits out of the hat

435
00:27:31,917 --> 00:27:33,852
- and prove someone else did it?
- I might.

436
00:27:34,419 --> 00:27:37,514
- I'd like to see that.
- Yeah. Maybe you will.

437
00:27:37,689 --> 00:27:43,959
Yeah. I think whoever blew up the
boat was actually trying to kill me.

438
00:27:44,563 --> 00:27:47,123
It was the same fellow who
broke in my house last night

439
00:27:47,299 --> 00:27:50,497
and slipped on a
pair of Rollerblades.

440
00:27:50,669 --> 00:27:52,001
Nearly broke his neck.

441
00:27:53,105 --> 00:27:54,505
You think it was me?

442
00:27:55,073 --> 00:27:56,473
Crossed my mind.

443
00:27:56,642 --> 00:27:58,577
Oh, no, no, no. Mr. Matlock, no.

444
00:27:58,744 --> 00:28:01,236
I was at home last night, alone.

445
00:28:01,413 --> 00:28:03,405
You've gotta believe me.

446
00:28:03,582 --> 00:28:05,414
I'm a changed man.

447
00:28:06,985 --> 00:28:10,353
Where were you yesterday
afternoon about 5:15

448
00:28:10,522 --> 00:28:12,047
when Ty Foster was killed?

449
00:28:12,958 --> 00:28:15,427
I'm sorry. Who is Ty Foster?

450
00:28:15,594 --> 00:28:18,996
Ty Foster is a fella who
unfortunately used to work for you.

451
00:28:19,164 --> 00:28:23,329
He was killed in Willow Springs,
and I wanna know where you were.

452
00:28:23,502 --> 00:28:26,404
Well, I don't think I know a
Ty Foster, but, uh, let me see.

453
00:28:26,572 --> 00:28:29,132
Yesterday at 5:15...

454
00:28:29,308 --> 00:28:31,334
I was involved in a car
accident over in Highgate

455
00:28:31,510 --> 00:28:32,910
between 4:30 and 5:00.

456
00:28:33,078 --> 00:28:34,444
You can check the police report.

457
00:28:35,747 --> 00:28:37,238
And let me see, at rush hour,

458
00:28:37,416 --> 00:28:41,285
that's gotta be at least 40
minutes from Willow Springs.

459
00:28:41,453 --> 00:28:43,354
I didn't kill anyone,
Mr. Matlock.

460
00:28:43,522 --> 00:28:45,923
I'm telling you, I'm
a changed man.

461
00:28:46,091 --> 00:28:47,719
I'm sure.

462
00:28:50,295 --> 00:28:52,560
I'll check with the police.

463
00:29:17,189 --> 00:29:20,318
- I've been waiting for you.
- Oh.

464
00:29:21,560 --> 00:29:26,999
I got the police report on Jeffrey
Spidel's accident yesterday.

465
00:29:27,933 --> 00:29:31,392
He couldn't have gotten from
Highgate to Willow Springs

466
00:29:31,570 --> 00:29:34,768
in time to kill Ty Foster.

467
00:29:34,940 --> 00:29:36,306
Hm.

468
00:29:36,475 --> 00:29:38,944
Well, remember the Baxter
University baseball cap

469
00:29:39,111 --> 00:29:40,909
they found next to
Ty Foster's body?

470
00:29:41,079 --> 00:29:42,513
Yeah.

471
00:29:43,015 --> 00:29:44,074
It wasn't his.

472
00:29:44,650 --> 00:29:48,178
The cap didn't fit his head,
and the hair inside wasn't his.

473
00:29:48,353 --> 00:29:50,083
Oh.

474
00:29:50,289 --> 00:29:51,814
What color was the hair?

475
00:29:53,025 --> 00:29:55,790
Black and short.

476
00:29:56,795 --> 00:30:01,733
I know a boy with black hair
who went to Baxter University.

477
00:30:02,501 --> 00:30:04,800
Do you think it was his hat?

478
00:30:05,604 --> 00:30:07,402
It could be,

479
00:30:07,572 --> 00:30:09,666
but he was convicted of murder.

480
00:30:11,877 --> 00:30:13,778
You have anything
to do with that?

481
00:30:15,080 --> 00:30:17,982
Oh, yeah. Yeah.

482
00:30:18,150 --> 00:30:21,018
<i>A couple years ago, I
agreed to give a lecture</i>

483
00:30:21,186 --> 00:30:23,815
<i>to the Law Club at
Baxter University.</i>

484
00:30:23,989 --> 00:30:28,552
<i>It turned out to be one of
the biggest mistakes of my life.</i>

485
00:30:29,761 --> 00:30:30,785
Yeah, Mark?

486
00:30:30,962 --> 00:30:33,090
Have you ever seen
the perfect murder?

487
00:30:33,265 --> 00:30:37,032
Well, I've seen cases
that were never solved.

488
00:30:37,202 --> 00:30:40,468
You really hate somebody
and you wanna kill them.

489
00:30:40,639 --> 00:30:42,369
- How you do it?
- Yeah. How you do it.

490
00:30:42,541 --> 00:30:46,979
How you do it. Theoretically,
if you wanted to kill somebody,

491
00:30:47,145 --> 00:30:49,580
uh, you'd first have
to have a motive.

492
00:30:49,748 --> 00:30:51,740
So you'd want to do it.

493
00:30:51,917 --> 00:30:55,615
Then you'd find
somebody to kill.

494
00:30:55,787 --> 00:30:58,484
- And you'd have to get rid of the body.
- Yeah.

495
00:30:58,657 --> 00:31:03,095
You'd get rid of the body and
then try to frame somebody else.

496
00:31:04,696 --> 00:31:06,426
<i>One of the girls in my class</i>

497
00:31:06,598 --> 00:31:10,501
<i>had just broken up with
a bully named Eddie Price</i>

498
00:31:10,669 --> 00:31:12,262
<i>who everybody hated.</i>

499
00:31:12,437 --> 00:31:15,168
We're over? After
six months? No.

500
00:31:15,340 --> 00:31:17,020
Hey, hey, you don't
seem to get it, do you?

501
00:31:17,175 --> 00:31:19,701
She doesn't wanna see you
anymore. Just get out of her way.

502
00:31:19,878 --> 00:31:21,403
You're beginning to annoy me.

503
00:31:21,580 --> 00:31:23,515
I said move it.

504
00:31:30,322 --> 00:31:32,553
- See you around.
- Mark.

505
00:31:32,724 --> 00:31:35,592
Leave me alone.
Just leave me alone.

506
00:32:01,887 --> 00:32:04,186
What the hell is going on?

507
00:32:04,356 --> 00:32:06,382
<i>Right after Eddie Price died,</i>

508
00:32:06,558 --> 00:32:08,891
<i>the dean of students
called me to his office</i>

509
00:32:09,060 --> 00:32:11,120
<i>and actually accused me</i>

510
00:32:11,530 --> 00:32:14,967
<i>of teaching those kids how
to commit a perfect murder.</i>

511
00:32:15,133 --> 00:32:17,932
Ben, I think you
had a hand in this.

512
00:32:18,870 --> 00:32:19,963
What did I do?

513
00:32:20,138 --> 00:32:23,575
You all but gave
your class a blueprint

514
00:32:23,742 --> 00:32:25,643
of how to commit
a perfect murder.

515
00:32:25,811 --> 00:32:28,508
The police think I'm in on this?

516
00:32:28,947 --> 00:32:31,382
Well, now, I didn't say
you did it intentionally.

517
00:32:31,550 --> 00:32:33,985
I'm saying I
didn't do it at all!

518
00:32:34,152 --> 00:32:36,417
Hey I saw you at
the café, Bobby.

519
00:32:36,588 --> 00:32:38,853
For all I know, you might
be trying to frame me.

520
00:32:39,024 --> 00:32:41,584
Bobby, I spoke to Buddy
Hart, Eddie's roommate.

521
00:32:41,760 --> 00:32:44,525
He said you hated Eddie
because he took Carol from you.

522
00:32:44,696 --> 00:32:47,461
Hated? Isn't that
a little overstated?

523
00:32:47,632 --> 00:32:49,726
Bobby, you said
you wanted to kill him.

524
00:32:49,901 --> 00:32:52,837
Time out here. Okay, I was madder
than hell when Carol first dumped me,

525
00:32:53,004 --> 00:32:54,199
but that was a long time ago.

526
00:32:54,372 --> 00:32:57,570
You may not believe this,
honey, but guys do get over you.

527
00:32:58,944 --> 00:33:01,184
You know what? I don't like
being treated like a suspect.

528
00:33:01,213 --> 00:33:02,306
Don't you get it?

529
00:33:02,481 --> 00:33:04,040
We're all suspects.

530
00:33:04,216 --> 00:33:07,812
<i>Fortunately, what I told this
group turned out to be true.</i>

531
00:33:07,986 --> 00:33:11,514
<i>There is no such thing
as a perfect murder.</i>

532
00:33:11,690 --> 00:33:17,391
Now, the night Eddie disappeared,
you were in your room studying.

533
00:33:17,562 --> 00:33:18,655
Yes.

534
00:33:18,830 --> 00:33:23,825
The proof we have of that is that
your car was parked down front.

535
00:33:24,002 --> 00:33:25,698
That's right, it was.

536
00:33:25,871 --> 00:33:30,935
Uh, well, how about Carol's
car that night? Did you drive it?

537
00:33:31,476 --> 00:33:35,538
No. I didn't. I told you
I was at home, so...

538
00:33:35,981 --> 00:33:38,143
Uh... Ahem.

539
00:33:40,519 --> 00:33:43,114
This is the gas
pedal off Carol's car.

540
00:33:43,288 --> 00:33:45,848
You see that shiny
spot right there?

541
00:33:46,558 --> 00:33:47,617
Yeah, I see it.

542
00:33:47,792 --> 00:33:49,158
I think that's varnish.

543
00:33:49,561 --> 00:33:51,257
What about you?

544
00:33:51,863 --> 00:33:53,126
- Could be.
- Yeah?

545
00:33:53,899 --> 00:33:54,958
And, uh...

546
00:33:56,902 --> 00:33:57,995
And this is, uh...

547
00:33:58,169 --> 00:34:00,263
What are you doing with my shoe?

548
00:34:00,939 --> 00:34:02,305
I borrowed it.

549
00:34:03,542 --> 00:34:04,840
What the hell is going on?

550
00:34:05,010 --> 00:34:07,010
I don't have to sit here
and answer your questions.

551
00:34:07,178 --> 00:34:10,012
- I'm out of here.
- Hey. Hey, Bob. Bob.

552
00:34:10,181 --> 00:34:12,173
- You got something to hide?
- No, but...

553
00:34:12,350 --> 00:34:14,717
Then sit down. Answer
the man's questions.

554
00:34:14,886 --> 00:34:19,756
You see this, uh, shiny spot here
on the bottom of your running shoe?

555
00:34:20,859 --> 00:34:23,454
- I see it.
- I think that's varnish too.

556
00:34:23,628 --> 00:34:26,689
I think it's the same varnish
that's on the gas pedal

557
00:34:26,865 --> 00:34:28,561
from Carol's car.

558
00:34:28,733 --> 00:34:31,567
Your uncle owns a
speedboat, doesn't he?

559
00:34:31,736 --> 00:34:34,706
An old, uh, mahogany inboard?

560
00:34:34,873 --> 00:34:36,501
Yeah, he does.

561
00:34:36,675 --> 00:34:42,478
Are you aware that
on, uh, September 30th

562
00:34:42,647 --> 00:34:46,084
at 5:00 in the evening,

563
00:34:46,251 --> 00:34:49,016
a fresh coat of varnish
was applied to that boat?

564
00:34:49,654 --> 00:34:51,816
No, I wasn't.

565
00:34:51,990 --> 00:34:57,361
I believe the
varnish on that boat

566
00:34:57,529 --> 00:34:59,760
is the same as the varnish
on your running shoe

567
00:34:59,931 --> 00:35:02,491
and the gas pedal
from Carol's car.

568
00:35:03,401 --> 00:35:04,835
That doesn't prove anything.

569
00:35:05,770 --> 00:35:09,002
Well, I think... I think you...

570
00:35:09,908 --> 00:35:11,672
I think you might
have killed Eddie.

571
00:35:12,877 --> 00:35:16,473
I think you might have slipped
out of your apartment that night.

572
00:35:16,648 --> 00:35:18,310
You didn't take your car

573
00:35:18,483 --> 00:35:22,318
because you knew that
somebody might notice it was gone.

574
00:35:22,487 --> 00:35:25,753
I think you might've used
the keys to Carol's car

575
00:35:25,924 --> 00:35:28,359
that she gave you
when you were dating.

576
00:35:28,860 --> 00:35:31,295
I think you may... Might
have driven her car

577
00:35:31,463 --> 00:35:34,831
out of the parking lot at the
library over to Eddie's house,

578
00:35:35,000 --> 00:35:36,730
killed him,

579
00:35:37,135 --> 00:35:40,867
put him in the car,
driven him to the boat,

580
00:35:41,673 --> 00:35:43,835
taken him out to sea

581
00:35:44,009 --> 00:35:45,534
and dumped his body overboard.

582
00:35:45,710 --> 00:35:49,147
You can't prove that
I killed Eddie Price.

583
00:35:49,314 --> 00:35:51,476
Mr. Matlock, you can't
prove that Eddie's dead.

584
00:35:51,650 --> 00:35:52,948
No.

585
00:35:54,185 --> 00:35:56,120
No, I can't.

586
00:35:57,122 --> 00:35:59,148
There's no body.

587
00:35:59,324 --> 00:36:00,815
There may never be a body.

588
00:36:01,826 --> 00:36:03,988
I was just making suppositions.

589
00:36:04,162 --> 00:36:07,792
But I tell you, as sure
as we're all sitting here,

590
00:36:08,800 --> 00:36:12,202
if I'm making
these suppositions,

591
00:36:12,370 --> 00:36:13,633
the police will too.

592
00:36:16,441 --> 00:36:17,773
Well, I'll find out where he is.

593
00:36:17,942 --> 00:36:19,706
I might still have
his parents' number.

594
00:36:19,878 --> 00:36:22,814
Let me see. Let me see.

595
00:36:37,929 --> 00:36:39,955
Uh, Miss Simpson?

596
00:36:40,131 --> 00:36:41,724
Ben Matlock.

597
00:36:41,900 --> 00:36:45,132
Uh, I was calling
about your son.

598
00:36:48,540 --> 00:36:50,805
No, I didn't.

599
00:36:51,009 --> 00:36:52,739
I'm sorry.

600
00:36:53,311 --> 00:36:54,836
Bye.

601
00:36:57,615 --> 00:36:58,708
What happened?

602
00:36:59,050 --> 00:37:03,181
Bobby Simpson got out
of prison four months ago.

603
00:37:03,354 --> 00:37:05,880
And six weeks ago,

604
00:37:06,491 --> 00:37:11,259
he was killed in an automobile
accident outside Miami.

605
00:37:12,530 --> 00:37:14,761
Drunk driving.

606
00:37:15,834 --> 00:37:20,135
Ben, if he's dead, what was
his hat doing at Ty Foster's?

607
00:37:21,206 --> 00:37:23,869
Jeffrey Spidel.

608
00:37:24,476 --> 00:37:28,379
He put it there.
He's toying with me.

609
00:37:28,813 --> 00:37:31,009
It has to be Jeffrey Spidel.

610
00:37:31,182 --> 00:37:34,675
Sure as I'm sitting
here, it's him.

611
00:37:49,634 --> 00:37:54,004
I'm pretty sure Judge
Stern's boat was over there.

612
00:37:55,039 --> 00:37:57,167
Hey, if you don't
mind me asking,

613
00:37:57,342 --> 00:37:59,174
what do you care
where we kept the boat?

614
00:37:59,344 --> 00:38:02,746
Oh. Uh, well, we figure the bomb
was put on the boat while it was here.

615
00:38:02,914 --> 00:38:04,746
Not that we've ever
blown up anything,

616
00:38:04,916 --> 00:38:08,182
but we figure the guy
had easier access on land

617
00:38:08,353 --> 00:38:11,084
- instead of in the water.
- Uh-huh.

618
00:38:11,289 --> 00:38:15,385
- Well, this is where it was.
- Huh.

619
00:38:15,560 --> 00:38:18,587
I'd have never known the
judge had his boat up here

620
00:38:18,763 --> 00:38:22,200
if he hadn't said he was
having it painted. Heh-heh.

621
00:38:34,279 --> 00:38:35,941
Um...

622
00:38:37,315 --> 00:38:39,841
Do you think you could
rope off this whole area

623
00:38:40,018 --> 00:38:42,078
for the rest of the day?

624
00:38:43,288 --> 00:38:46,884
- I guess.
- Yeah. Yeah.

625
00:38:47,091 --> 00:38:48,616
Yeah.

626
00:38:52,096 --> 00:38:58,093
Mr. Spidel, two years ago,
I accused you of murder,

627
00:38:58,269 --> 00:39:01,603
and ultimately, you were
convicted. Remember that?

628
00:39:01,773 --> 00:39:04,868
Yes, I was in prison for two years
before the case was thrown out.

629
00:39:05,043 --> 00:39:06,443
How do you feel about me

630
00:39:06,611 --> 00:39:10,275
since I was the one responsible
for sending you to prison?

631
00:39:10,448 --> 00:39:13,816
Well, I think you were
just doing your job.

632
00:39:13,985 --> 00:39:16,420
- No animosity?
- No, sir. None.

633
00:39:17,555 --> 00:39:19,820
You ever feel like killing me?

634
00:39:19,991 --> 00:39:22,085
Mr. Matlock, I'm
not angry with you.

635
00:39:22,260 --> 00:39:23,728
I have come to
terms with all that.

636
00:39:23,895 --> 00:39:27,696
I don't believe
that for a second.

637
00:39:28,233 --> 00:39:31,795
Didn't you hire Ty Foster
to mug my friend Will Owens

638
00:39:31,970 --> 00:39:34,269
so we would have to
cancel our fishing trip?

639
00:39:34,672 --> 00:39:37,301
And didn't you have
Ty Foster take my client,

640
00:39:37,475 --> 00:39:40,035
Glen Silver, to
lunch at the marina

641
00:39:40,211 --> 00:39:42,043
so his fingerprints
would be found

642
00:39:42,213 --> 00:39:44,910
in the vicinity of
Judge Stern's boat slip?

643
00:39:45,083 --> 00:39:48,019
I told you, sir, I do
not know a Ty Foster.

644
00:39:48,186 --> 00:39:49,814
As always, you were smart.

645
00:39:49,988 --> 00:39:52,890
You did everything just right.

646
00:39:53,057 --> 00:39:56,755
You chose Glen Silver to
frame for the judge's murder

647
00:39:56,928 --> 00:40:01,059
because you knew that Glen
blamed the judge for disbarring him.

648
00:40:01,232 --> 00:40:05,966
And the murder took place on
the anniversary of the disbarment.

649
00:40:06,137 --> 00:40:10,233
You had everything pointed
at the judge as the target,

650
00:40:11,242 --> 00:40:14,110
when all the time

651
00:40:14,279 --> 00:40:16,839
you were trying to
murder me, weren't you?

652
00:40:17,015 --> 00:40:18,135
I don't wanna murder anyone.

653
00:40:18,283 --> 00:40:19,803
It should've worked.
Should've worked.

654
00:40:19,951 --> 00:40:23,752
I went fishing with the
judge, the bomb went off,

655
00:40:23,922 --> 00:40:26,517
but I got seasick
and got off the boat

656
00:40:26,691 --> 00:40:27,989
before it happened.

657
00:40:28,159 --> 00:40:30,560
Now the judge is dead.

658
00:40:30,728 --> 00:40:31,957
Ty Foster works for you,

659
00:40:32,130 --> 00:40:33,996
and he's probably
figured out what's going on.

660
00:40:34,165 --> 00:40:36,361
So would you just tell
the court where you were

661
00:40:36,534 --> 00:40:39,766
at 5:15 p.m., January 15th.

662
00:40:41,172 --> 00:40:44,665
Well, as I told you, I was involved
in a car accident in Highgate.

663
00:40:44,842 --> 00:40:45,935
It was rush hour.

664
00:40:46,110 --> 00:40:50,377
You were about 45 minutes from
where you were to Ty Foster's house

665
00:40:50,548 --> 00:40:53,313
when he was...
When he was killed.

666
00:40:53,484 --> 00:40:58,616
This, uh, Baxter
University baseball cap

667
00:40:58,790 --> 00:41:01,055
was found near the body.

668
00:41:01,259 --> 00:41:04,388
It apparently belonged
to, uh, Bobby Simpson,

669
00:41:04,562 --> 00:41:08,397
another young man I was
responsible for sending to prison.

670
00:41:08,566 --> 00:41:10,432
Ever seen it before?

671
00:41:10,601 --> 00:41:13,571
Mm... No, I don't think so. No.

672
00:41:14,706 --> 00:41:17,801
Bobby was killed
about six weeks ago.

673
00:41:17,976 --> 00:41:20,002
Car accident.

674
00:41:20,178 --> 00:41:21,646
Of course you knew
that, didn't you?

675
00:41:23,247 --> 00:41:24,545
No.

676
00:41:24,716 --> 00:41:26,150
I think you did.

677
00:41:26,317 --> 00:41:30,584
Get this. Somebody stole this
cap and placed it by the body.

678
00:41:30,755 --> 00:41:33,725
- It was you, wasn't it?
- No.

679
00:41:33,891 --> 00:41:37,259
I think it was. You
were toying with me.

680
00:41:37,428 --> 00:41:40,694
You were waiting your
chance to get back at me.

681
00:41:40,865 --> 00:41:46,634
When you stole his cap, you
also stole Bobby's motorcycle.

682
00:41:46,804 --> 00:41:50,639
That's how you got across
town in rush-hour traffic.

683
00:41:50,808 --> 00:41:53,573
You had the car
accident on purpose.

684
00:41:53,978 --> 00:41:55,674
You had the motorcycle
hidden right there.

685
00:41:55,847 --> 00:41:59,807
All you had to do was hop on it,
rush over to Ty's house and kill him.

686
00:41:59,984 --> 00:42:02,180
- Right?
- No.

687
00:42:02,353 --> 00:42:03,616
Objection. Irrelevant.

688
00:42:03,788 --> 00:42:06,189
Your Honor, possibly
Mr. Matlock is confused.

689
00:42:06,357 --> 00:42:08,121
He's trying the wrong case.

690
00:42:08,693 --> 00:42:13,825
Your Honor, I apologize
for this digression.

691
00:42:13,998 --> 00:42:16,126
But this man is a
cold-blooded killer.

692
00:42:16,300 --> 00:42:19,930
And in time it will come
out that he killed Ty Foster.

693
00:42:20,371 --> 00:42:23,967
But I intend to prove
that he killed Judge Stern.

694
00:42:24,375 --> 00:42:26,071
- Make it snappy, Mr. Matlock.
- Yes, sir.

695
00:42:29,347 --> 00:42:32,579
- You like boats, Mr. Spidel?
- Not particularly. No.

696
00:42:32,750 --> 00:42:34,218
- Ever own one?
- No.

697
00:42:34,385 --> 00:42:39,449
So you don't go to places like
marinas and boat shows and dry docks?

698
00:42:39,624 --> 00:42:40,648
No, no, never.

699
00:42:40,825 --> 00:42:44,626
What would you say if I
told you I had an eyewitness

700
00:42:44,796 --> 00:42:46,958
who saw you at the
Harbor Dry Dock?

701
00:42:47,865 --> 00:42:49,629
Well, I'd say he was a liar.

702
00:42:49,801 --> 00:42:51,394
Heh. Yeah, you'd be right.

703
00:42:51,569 --> 00:42:53,800
But I've got something better.

704
00:42:53,971 --> 00:42:57,339
I don't know. Maybe it's
easier to put a bomb on a boat

705
00:42:57,508 --> 00:43:00,876
when it's up on blocks
than in the water.

706
00:43:01,045 --> 00:43:05,983
But I decided I'd make
a trip to Harbor Dry Dock

707
00:43:06,150 --> 00:43:08,585
where Judge Stern's
boat was painted.

708
00:43:08,753 --> 00:43:11,780
And there was paint
all around on the ground,

709
00:43:11,956 --> 00:43:14,516
and you know how people
are, can't keep them out of it.

710
00:43:14,692 --> 00:43:20,632
There was shoe prints here and
there, different shoes, different sizes.

711
00:43:20,798 --> 00:43:24,701
And, uh, the police...

712
00:43:26,671 --> 00:43:30,073
took this pair of shoes

713
00:43:30,241 --> 00:43:32,506
from your place this morning.

714
00:43:32,677 --> 00:43:35,442
And this is what the
police report says:

715
00:43:35,613 --> 00:43:40,813
The report states that this
paint on the bottom of your shoe

716
00:43:40,985 --> 00:43:43,614
is an exact match with
the blue bottom paint

717
00:43:43,788 --> 00:43:46,314
used at the Harbor Dry Dock.

718
00:43:46,791 --> 00:43:50,626
It also says that this
shoe is an exact match

719
00:43:50,795 --> 00:43:55,062
with a print found in the
paint at the Harbor Dry Dock

720
00:43:55,233 --> 00:43:59,728
right next to where Judge
Stern's boat was kept.

721
00:44:00,605 --> 00:44:03,268
You were there, Mr. Spidel.

722
00:44:04,041 --> 00:44:05,202
Probably at night.

723
00:44:05,376 --> 00:44:06,844
You couldn't see the paint.

724
00:44:07,245 --> 00:44:11,182
You planted the bomb
that killed the judge.

725
00:44:11,349 --> 00:44:15,878
And you tried to kill me again.

726
00:44:16,988 --> 00:44:20,322
And this time, if I have
anything to do with it,

727
00:44:20,491 --> 00:44:24,622
I'm gonna send you to
prison for the rest of your life.

728
00:44:31,102 --> 00:44:38,066
